*(
    title: Responses to Pathogens
    description: What a plant does when infected, and what an animal's first lines of defence do.
    tags: pathogen response, plant defence, innate immunity, barriers, module 7, biology, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Most encounters with a pathogen never become an illness, because the first defences are non-specific, already in place, and effective against almost anything. They get less attention than the immune response proper and do most of the work.

    Written out here: physical and chemical barriers, the plant responses that operate without any circulating immune system at all, and the innate animal response — inflammation, phagocytosis and fever — which acts within minutes and treats every intruder the same way.

    When the non-specific defences are not enough, a slower system takes over, one that targets a particular pathogen and remembers it. That is *Immunity*.

    internote(ref: immunity.chalk)
}
